WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.050
For most people, going to the movies is a
surefire form of stress relief one that's

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:09.269
specifically intended to distract us from
the turmoil of our own lives for a least an

00:00:09.269 --> 00:00:10.269
hour or two.

00:00:10.269 --> 00:00:14.420
Of course, that can occasionally backfire,
because some films just won't allow you to

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:18.650
switch off your brain and check out of the
world for even one single minute.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:20.390
That's not always a bad thing, though.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:24.330
In fact, some of the best films out there
are good simply because they challenge our

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:25.520
hearts and minds.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.500
Here are some of the most stressful movies
you'll still want to watch.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:30.500
Seven

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:36.440
When Seven made its way to theaters back in
the fall of 1995, the world was simply not

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:37.450
ready.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:42.480
But David Fincher's relentlessly bleak symphony
of sorrows is still a masterpiece that continues

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.580
to serve as a bizarre, gory mirror for moral
and societal excesses.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:52.870
The film opens on a dour note and only gets
more desolate, desperate, and stressful with

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:54.610
every moment that follows.

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:59.850
Still, Seven is so meticulously constructed,
obsessively executed, and skillfully acted

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:04.780
by its leads that even at its most gruesome
it's almost impossible not to want to watch

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:05.780
it all unfold.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.789
"Awwww, what's in the box?

00:01:07.789 --> 00:01:09.950
What's in the f------ box?"

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:14.380
Whether it's because of Seven's obsessive
nature, it remains one of the most rewatchable

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:18.400
movies ever made, no matter how difficult
it is.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.810
mother!

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:25.189
Speaking of directors who like to push buttons,
Darren Aronofsky is another director known

00:01:25.189 --> 00:01:26.460
to break boundaries.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.740
But nothing in his riveting collection could've
prepared audiences for the brain-bomb that

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.960
was his 2017 offering, mother!

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.619
Set in a tranquil, secluded country home,
the film follows a struggling poet and his

00:01:38.619 --> 00:01:41.859
young wife as they try to write and remodel
their home.

00:01:41.859 --> 00:01:46.490
That peaceful existence is disturbed with
the arrival of unexpected guests, and things

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.320
quickly get out of hand.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:53.549
As mother! propels itself to its shocking
but inevitable finale, the moments that lead

00:01:53.549 --> 00:01:57.460
us there come with a constant, feverish upping
of the ante.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:01.829
Watching mother! is an emotionally draining
affair, which might explain the movie's box

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:03.060
office problems.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.850
But like the best of Aronofsky's films, the
anxiety and discomfort caused by watching

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:10.800
it is still totally worth it.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.180
Buried

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.580
For much of his early career, the thought
of taking Ryan Reynolds seriously as an actor

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.420
just seemed laughable.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:22.900
But many filmgoers' opinions of Reynolds as
an actor were forever changed after seeing

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:27.390
his nerve-wracking 2010 micro-budget thriller
Buried.

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:32.300
The whole film takes place in a coffin buried
somewhere in the Iraqi desert, and Reynolds

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:36.190
is the sole actor and yes, he's buried inside
the coffin.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:40.310
If you're claustrophobic, sitting through
this movie is a lot like subjecting yourself

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:42.470
to a 95-minute anxiety attack.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:47.650
And even if you're not afraid of enclosed
spaces, Buried is still tough to tangle with.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:51.560
But if you can make it through the cramped
space of the film, it's a first-rate thriller

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:56.680
with a few clever twists, some surprisingly
insightful commentary, and an impressive dramatic

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:01.790
performance from Ryan Reynolds, who doesn't
snidely snicker or break the third wall even

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:03.060
once.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:04.500
Under the Skin

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:09.400
Sometimes it's hard to pinpoint exactly what
it is about a movie that stresses you out,

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.700
but in the case of Jonathan Glazer's Under
the Skin, it's quite literally everything.

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:19.430
The film's photography, music, acting, sound
design, and editing are designed with the

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:21.930
single goal of keeping viewers on edge.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:27.570
The result is a challenging, deeply disturbing
movie that you don't really watch as much

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:28.950
as you experience.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:33.940
At the same time, though, it's also a profoundly
engaging, ambitious picture that hypnotizes

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:39.620
viewers into entering its darkness as it examines
complex topics of identity, extraterrestrial

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.410
morality, and what it means to be human.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:47.321
At the center of that exploration is the single
weirdest, and possibly strongest, performance

00:03:47.321 --> 00:03:49.220
of Scarlett Johansson's career.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:54.120
The film works best if you know as little
as possible going in, but watchers should

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:59.780
be warned that if you feel uneasy while watching
the flick, that just means it's working.

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:00.780
Enemy

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:06.040
Denis Villeneuve's doppelgänger thriller
Enemy is adapted from a novel by experimental

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:11.810
novelist José Saramago and follows an average
man whose middling life is thrown into flux

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:14.520
when he spots his exact double in a movie.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:19.400
What follows is a study of identity and modern
isolation that finds Jake Gyllenhaal in top

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:24.460
form playing dual roles, and Villeneuve beginning
to tease the daring style that has become

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:26.069
his trademark in the years since.

00:04:26.069 --> 00:04:30.719
It's also worth noting that spiders figure
prominently in the film, which means there's

00:04:30.719 --> 00:04:34.560
a fair amount of arachnid angst involved in
watching it.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:38.961
More importantly, though, the film is also
rife with metaphorical webs that the director

00:04:38.961 --> 00:04:43.300
uses to pull viewers further into the sticky,
deadly center.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:48.629
Enemy is one of those rare movies that's distressing
enough just to watch, and even more so after

00:04:48.629 --> 00:04:53.669
the credits have rolled and you're stuck trying
to figure out what the hell just happened.

00:04:53.669 --> 00:04:54.889
Bug

00:04:54.889 --> 00:05:00.419
William Friedkin directed one of the most
unnerving films ever made with 2006's Bug,

00:05:00.419 --> 00:05:02.410
but odds are you've never heard of it.

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:07.509
Bug features one of the finest performances
of Ashley Judd's career and a breakout turn

00:05:07.509 --> 00:05:09.280
from the great Michael Shannon.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.979
Perhaps one of the reasons Bug hasn't quite
broken any box office records is that it's

00:05:13.979 --> 00:05:15.630
hardly a feel-good affair.

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:20.130
The bulk of the film is set inside a dingy
hotel room and follows a damaged woman who

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:24.719
seems to be on the verge of a fresh start
after meeting a kind war vet.

00:05:24.719 --> 00:05:30.110
When her new pal turns out to be one deeply
unhinged individual, what follows is a terrifying

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.090
descent into madness.

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:36.830
"I am the super mother bugggggggggggggg"

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:41.410
Friedkin keeps the action taut throughout,
making the proceedings extra stressful by

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:46.560
using expressionistic lighting, shooting his
stars in unflattering close-ups, and featuring

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.909
a handful of body horror scenes that will
definitely make your skin crawl.

00:05:50.909 --> 00:05:55.990
Bug is essentially the cinematic equivalent
of poking at a raw nerve for a couple of hours,

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:56.990
but it's well worth the pain.

